{"id":"math-120-lecture-4","course":"MATH 120","kind":"lecture","lecture":4,"title":"Sequences & Series","questions":[{"id":"l4-q1","prompt":"Sequences & series are framed as leading naturally toward:","choices":["A The concept of a limit (foundation for calculus).","B Only geometry proofs.","C Spelling bees.","D Music theory alone."],"answer":0,"cite":"4.2","explain":"Bridge to limits."},{"id":"l4-q2","prompt":"An arithmetic sequence changes by a:","choices":["A Common difference (adding repeatedly).","B Common ratio only.","C Random jump.","D Derivative."],"answer":0,"cite":"4.19","explain":"Common difference +8 example."},{"id":"l4-q3","prompt":"A geometric sequence changes by a:","choices":["A Common ratio (multiplying repeatedly).","B Common difference only.","C Sine wave.","D Matrix."],"answer":0,"cite":"4.31","explain":"Common ratio form."},{"id":"l4-q4","prompt":"A series differs from a sequence because a series is about:","choices":["A Summing the terms.","B Listing without adding.","C Only the last term.","D Graphs of ellipses."],"answer":0,"cite":"4.43","explain":"Series = sum of terms."},{"id":"l4-q5","prompt":"Partial sums S_n help understand infinite series by looking at:","choices":["A Finite running totals as n grows.","B Only the first term.","C Derivatives.","D Angles."],"answer":0,"cite":"4.49","explain":"Partial sums as accessible objects."}]}